From: quickjs-ng bundle work
Subject: Register VM: frameless call classifiers (0189)

What this does
  Splits the lazy-materialize branch out of the register VM's `call` and
  `call_method` threaded classifiers into separate noinline handlers
  (`regvm_t_call_lazy`, `regvm_t_call_method_lazy`). The classifier is now a
  tag test, a class-id test, a ready-byte test and a jump; the materialize call
  -- and the stack frame the compiler has to keep across it -- is reached only
  for a lazy callee that is not yet record-eligible.

Why we need it
  Inlining `js_materialize_function_body` into the classifier forced the
  classifier to be a calling function, so every dispatch of a JS call paid the
  prologue. On aarch64 that is the callee-saved save/restore (the classifier
  was framed even though the call is on a branch no eligible call takes); on
  x86 it is a stack-alignment `push %rax`/`pop %rax`. Measured on x86
  (clang-19, threaded+record), the split retires 3 fewer instructions per JS
  call, uniform across 0/1/4-argument and method forms (60,000,000 over the
  20M-call micro), and box2d `fixed.js` drops 43.7M instructions. Cycles are
  neutral.

Gates
  box2d checksum 546.1008391043414; differential suite all pass, none skipped;
  test262 at baseline; ASan clean. The handler frame check in
  `tools/regvm/gate.sh` now also asserts the two classifiers contain no
  push/pop (the [sp]-based check cannot see an x86 alignment push), so the
  regression cannot return.

No bytecode change:
  No. Runtime only.

